{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Follow-up recorded by ae96-f114-5363-4d57, which brought `scripts/` under `ruff check` and `ruff format` but NOT under mypy.\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`Makefile:241-242` defines `typecheck:` as `mypy src/rebar`, while `Makefile:13` already sets `sources = src tests scripts` for ruff only. So the 47 `.py` files under `scripts/` are lint-clean but type-unchecked. `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:108` runs `make typecheck` as the gating \"mypy — full library\" step behind the `Verified` vote, and that same workflow invokes several `scripts/*.py` gate implementations directly (e.g. `scripts/check_verify_gate_parity.py`, referenced at `_build-and-test.yml:124`). The programs that enforce the `Verified` vote are themselves outside the type gate.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Decision: include `scripts/` in the typecheck scope** rather than excluding it. Widen the `typecheck` target to `mypy src/rebar scripts`, then fix the type errors it surfaces in the script sources themselves — no per-module mypy override, no blanket `# type: ignore`.\n\nThis is the honest option because the measured error volume is small: running `mypy src/rebar scripts` in this checkout reports **15 errors in 6 files** out of 490 files checked (`scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` x7, `scripts/plan_review_impact_distribution.py` x4, plus one each in `scripts/capture_jira_fixtures.py`, `scripts/probe_rebar.py`, `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py`, `scripts/t5c_calibrate.py`). Every one is a missing container annotation or a lost `X | None` narrowing — several are latent bugs (e.g. `capture_jira_fixtures.py:221` calls `.strip()` on a `str | None`, `check_complexity_baseline.py:574` passes `dict[str, int] | None` where `dict[str, int]` is required). Relaxing flags for `scripts/` would suppress exactly the findings worth having.\n\nae96 flagged the `sys.path` bootstraps (9 files under `scripts/` mutate `sys.path`) as the structural obstacle. Verified empirically that they are already accommodated by existing config and need no change: `pyproject.toml` sets `follow_imports = \"silent\"` (errors are reported only for files named on the command line) and `ignore_missing_imports = true`, so a path-injected import degrades to `Any` instead of erroring. None of the 15 errors is an import error.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `Makefile` — `typecheck` recipe becomes `mypy src/rebar scripts`; update its help text/comment to say the scope now includes `scripts/`.\n- `scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` — type the `_resolve_argv`/`_resolve_elements` opaque-sentinel return and the `ast` node params so `.lineno`/`.value` resolve; drop the two now-unused `# type: ignore` comments.\n- `scripts/plan_review_impact_distribution.py` — annotate the bare `collections.Counter()` locals and key `tier_sev` with a `str` key.\n- `scripts/capture_jira_fixtures.py` — narrow `value` before `.strip()`.\n- `scripts/probe_rebar.py` — annotate `task_row`.\n- `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py` — preserve the `base is None` narrowing at the `lock_against_base` call.\n- `scripts/t5c_calibrate.py` — make `plan_text` return `str`.\n- `tests/unit/test_typecheck_scope.py` (new) — guard that `scripts/` stays in the `typecheck` recipe.\n\n## Testing\n\nNew guard test `tests/unit/test_typecheck_scope.py`, modelled on the existing `tests/unit/test_ci_actionlint_scope.py` (which parses the `lint:` recipe out of the Makefile the same way):\n\n- Happy path: parse the `typecheck:` target's recipe from `Makefile` and assert it invokes `mypy` with both `src/rebar` and `scripts` as arguments.\n- Failure path: assert the guard actually fails on a re-narrowed recipe — feed the recipe parser a synthetic `typecheck:\\n\\tmypy src/rebar` body and assert the scope assertion rejects it, so the test cannot silently pass if the target is later reverted.\n\n`make typecheck` itself is the end-to-end check that the widened scope is green.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The include-vs-exclude decision is recorded durably in the tree: the `Makefile` `typecheck` target carries a comment naming this ticket and stating that `scripts/` is in scope and why — verified by reading `Makefile`.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` runs mypy over `scripts/` as well as `src/rebar` — verified by `grep 'mypy src/rebar scripts' Makefile` and by `make typecheck` output reporting the scripts files as checked.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` exits 0 with `scripts/` in scope — verified by running it in the worktree venv.\n- [ ] No new `# type: ignore` comment and no `[[tool.mypy.overrides]]` block relaxing strictness for `scripts/` is added — verified by `git diff` showing zero added `type: ignore` lines and no `pyproject.toml` mypy-override change.\n- [ ] The CI-invoked scripts are covered, since directory-level `mypy scripts` names every `scripts/**/*.py` — verified by `make typecheck` reporting a checked-file count that includes all 47 script files.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_typecheck_scope.py` passes and fails on a re-narrowed recipe — verified by `pytest tests/unit/test_typecheck_scope.py -q`.\n- [ ] `make lint` is green — verified by running it in the worktree venv."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786306937644786001,"uuid":"1f70db1e-ca01-4445-b908-cf76c8095307"}